[FINAL-TEXT] All pings succeed and router1 has all four interfaces UP with correct IP addresses. The network is fully healthy. I now have enough evidence for a definitive diagnosis.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported outage does not exist in the live lab. Every element of the alleged failure was methodically verified and found healthy:

**Router1 (the "dead router")** is fully operational. All four interfaces are UP with correct IP assignments: eth0 = 192.168.3.1/24 (Office LAN), eth1 = 192.168.4.1/24 (Principle LAN), eth2 = 10.10.0.2/8 (WAN to router0), eth3 = 20.20.0.1/8 (WAN to router2). No interface is in a down or missing state.

**RIP routing is fully converged** on all three routers. Router1 holds complete routes to 1.0.0.0/8 (Servers), 128.168.0.0/16 (Internet Lab), 192.168.1.0/24 (IT), and 192.168.2.0/24 (CS) — all learned via RIP from neighbors and installed in the FIB. Router0 and Router2 likewise carry full tables with correct next-hops.

**End-to-end connectivity from the Office LAN is intact.** Direct pings from `office_pc` (192.168.3.x) to every claimed-unreachable destination all returned 0% packet loss: Server Room (pc1 / 1.0.0.5), Internet Lab (pc2 / 128.168.0.2), IT dept (it_hod / 192.168.1.2), CS dept (cs_hod / 192.168.2.2), and Principle area (laptop0 / 192.168.4.3).

**Root cause:** There is no fault. The ticket describes a false premise — the reporter's perception of an outage does not correspond to any current network condition. No corrective action is required; the network is operating normally.
=== END ===